  <section class="section" id="s1">
    <div class="section-tag">Introduction</div>
    <h2>1. What Is a Cybersecurity Home Lab?</h2>

    <p>
      Imagine a scientist who wants to study viruses safely. They don't release the virus into the city — they build a sealed, controlled laboratory where experiments can be run without risk to the outside world. A <strong>cybersecurity home lab</strong> is exactly that: a private, isolated environment on your personal computer where you can safely practice hacking techniques, test defences, and learn how real-world attacks work — all without touching any live system you don't own.
    </p>
    <p>
      The home lab is built using <strong>virtual machines (VMs)</strong> — software-simulated computers that run inside your real computer. You get a complete, fully functional operating system in a window, with its own hard drive, RAM, and network card, yet everything stays contained within your host machine.
    </p>

    <div class="analogy-box">
      <div class="analogy-label">💡 Analogy</div>
      <p style="margin:0">Think of your computer as a large apartment building. Each virtual machine is a separate flat inside it. The flats share the building's electricity and plumbing (hardware), but each tenant lives in isolation — whatever happens in Flat 3 doesn't spill into Flat 5. VirtualBox is the building manager who sets the rules for each flat.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Why Does Every Security Learner Need a Lab?</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Safe experimentation:</strong> Run malware, exploits, and attack tools without harming real systems.</li>
      <li><strong>Legal compliance:</strong> You own the target machines — so attacking them is perfectly legal.</li>
      <li><strong>Repeatability:</strong> Restore a broken VM in seconds using snapshots; try the same attack 100 times.</li>
      <li><strong>Cost-effective:</strong> All the major tools are free and open-source.</li>
      <li><strong>Career readiness:</strong> Most certifications (CEH, OSCP, CompTIA Security+) require hands-on lab work.</li>
    </ul>

