  <section class="section" id="s2">
    <div class="section-tag">Core Tool</div>
    <h2>2. VirtualBox — The Foundation</h2>

    <h3>What Is VirtualBox?</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>VirtualBox</strong> is a free, open-source <em>hypervisor</em> developed by Oracle Corporation. A hypervisor is software that lets one physical machine run multiple virtual machines simultaneously. VirtualBox is classified as a <strong>Type 2 hypervisor</strong>, meaning it runs on top of a host operating system (Windows, macOS, or Linux) rather than directly on hardware.
    </p>

    <div class="info-card accent-left">
      <h4>Quick History of VirtualBox</h4>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>2007:</strong> Released by Innotek GmbH as an open-source project.</li>
        <li><strong>2008:</strong> Acquired by Sun Microsystems.</li>
        <li><strong>2010:</strong> Sun acquired by Oracle — VirtualBox becomes "Oracle VM VirtualBox."</li>
        <li><strong>2023–present:</strong> Actively maintained, supporting modern CPUs and ARM architecture.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Type 1 vs Type 2 Hypervisors</h3>
    <div class="compare-grid">
      <div class="info-card">
        <h5>Type 1 (Bare-Metal)</h5>
        <ul>
          <li>Runs directly on hardware</li>
          <li>Examples: VMware ESXi, Microsoft Hyper-V, Xen</li>
          <li>Used in enterprise data centres</li>
          <li>Better performance, more complex setup</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="info-card">
        <h5>Type 2 (Hosted)</h5>
        <ul>
          <li>Runs on top of an existing OS</li>
          <li>Examples: VirtualBox, VMware Workstation</li>
          <li>Perfect for home labs and learning</li>
          <li>Slightly slower but easy to manage</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Key VirtualBox Concepts</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Host:</strong> Your real physical machine running the hypervisor.</li>
      <li><strong>Guest:</strong> The virtual machine running inside VirtualBox.</li>
      <li><strong>Virtual Disk Image (.vdi):</strong> A file on your host that acts as the guest's hard drive.</li>
      <li><strong>Guest Additions:</strong> A package installed inside the VM that enables clipboard sharing, drag-and-drop, and better display resolution.</li>
      <li><strong>Snapshot:</strong> A frozen point-in-time copy of the VM's state — your undo button.</li>
      <li><strong>NAT, Host-Only, Bridged:</strong> Different networking modes that control how the VM connects to the world.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>System Requirements</h3>
    <div class="info-card accent2-left">
      <ul>
        <li><strong>CPU:</strong> Intel or AMD with hardware virtualisation (VT-x / AMD-V) — enabled in BIOS/UEFI.</li>
        <li><strong>RAM:</strong> Minimum 8 GB (16 GB strongly recommended — each VM needs its own allocation).</li>
        <li><strong>Storage:</strong> At least 60 GB of free disk space for two VMs.</li>
        <li><strong>OS:</strong> Windows 10/11, macOS 10.15+, or any modern Linux distribution.</li>
        <li><strong>Internet:</strong> Required for downloading ISOs and updates (not for the lab network itself).</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

