  <section class="section" id="s3">
    <div class="section-tag">Installation</div>
    <h2>3. Installing &amp; Configuring VirtualBox</h2>

    <h3>Step-by-Step Installation</h3>
    <ol class="step-list">
      <li>
        <div><strong>Download VirtualBox</strong> — Visit <code>virtualbox.org/wiki/Downloads</code> and download the installer for your host OS. Also download the <strong>Extension Pack</strong> (same page) — it adds USB 2.0/3.0 support and remote desktop access.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div><strong>Run the installer</strong> — Accept defaults. On Windows, it will temporarily disconnect your network adapters while installing virtual NICs — this is normal.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div><strong>Enable hardware virtualisation in BIOS</strong> — Restart your machine and enter BIOS/UEFI (usually Del, F2, or F12 on boot). Look for <em>Intel VT-x</em>, <em>AMD-V</em>, or <em>SVM Mode</em> and enable it. Save and exit.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div><strong>Install the Extension Pack</strong> — Open VirtualBox → File → Preferences → Extensions → click the "+" icon → select the downloaded <code>.vbox-extpack</code> file → install.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div><strong>Verify installation</strong> — Open VirtualBox. You should see the main manager window with a "New" button in the top left. You're ready to create VMs.</div>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Creating a New Virtual Machine (General Steps)</h3>
    <ol class="step-list">
      <li><div>Click <strong>New</strong> → give the VM a name (e.g., "Kali-Attacker") → choose type (Linux) and version (Debian 64-bit).</div></li>
      <li><div>Allocate <strong>RAM</strong>: 2048 MB minimum for Kali; 1024 MB for Metasploitable. Never exceed half your host RAM.</div></li>
      <li><div>Create a <strong>Virtual Hard Disk</strong>: VDI format, dynamically allocated, 20 GB for Kali, 10 GB for target.</div></li>
      <li><div>Under <strong>Settings → Storage</strong>, attach the ISO file to the optical drive (Controller: IDE).</div></li>
      <li><div>Configure <strong>Network adapters</strong> (covered in detail in Section 4).</div></li>
      <li><div>Start the VM and follow the OS installation wizard.</div></li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Important VM Settings to Tune</h3>
    <div class="info-card">
      <ul>
        <li><strong>CPU cores:</strong> Settings → System → Processor → assign 2 CPUs for Kali. Do not exceed the green safe zone shown in the slider.</li>
        <li><strong>Video memory:</strong> Settings → Display → 128 MB, enable 3D acceleration if supported.</li>
        <li><strong>Clipboard:</strong> Settings → General → Advanced → Shared Clipboard: Bidirectional (after Guest Additions install).</li>
        <li><strong>Boot order:</strong> Settings → System → Motherboard → put Optical first (for ISO install), then Hard Disk.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

