  <!-- ── SECTION 4 ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="section" id="s4">
    <div class="section-tag">Networking</div>
    <h2>4. Networking in VirtualBox — NAT vs Host-Only</h2>

    <p>
      Network configuration is <em>the most critical part</em> of your home lab. Get it wrong and either your VMs can't communicate, or worse, your attack traffic leaks onto a real network. VirtualBox offers five networking modes. For our lab we use exactly two.
    </p>

    <h3>The Five VirtualBox Network Modes</h3>

    <div class="info-card accent-left">
      <h4>1. NAT (Network Address Translation)</h4>
      <p>The VM shares the host's internet connection through a virtual NAT router. The VM can reach the internet, but nothing outside can reach the VM. Think of it like a hotel room — you can browse the web, but nobody on the internet can call your room directly.</p>
      <p><strong>Use case in the lab:</strong> Assign this to Kali's Adapter 1 so it can download tool updates from the internet.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="info-card accent2-left">
      <h4>2. Host-Only Adapter</h4>
      <p>Creates a completely private network between the host and all VMs that use it. No internet access. No access to other real networks. Perfect isolation. VirtualBox creates a virtual network interface on your host (e.g., <code>vboxnet0</code>) and assigns IPs in the <code>192.168.56.0/24</code> range by default.</p>
      <p><strong>Use case in the lab:</strong> Assign this to ALL VMs (both Kali and the target). This is their battle network — completely isolated from the real world.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="info-card">
      <h4>3. Bridged Adapter</h4>
      <p>The VM appears as a real machine on your local network, getting an IP from your home router. <strong>NEVER use this for your target VMs</strong> — it puts vulnerable machines on your real network.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="info-card">
      <h4>4. Internal Network</h4>
      <p>Like Host-Only but even more isolated — the host itself cannot communicate with these VMs. Useful for completely air-gapped simulations.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="info-card">
      <h4>5. NAT Network</h4>
      <p>A variation of NAT that allows multiple VMs to communicate with <em>each other</em> through the NAT, while still sharing host internet. Useful but less commonly used in beginner labs.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Recommended Lab Configuration</h3>
    <div class="ascii-diagram">
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    KALI LINUX VM                        │
│                                                         │
│  Adapter 1: NAT           Adapter 2: Host-Only          │
│  (eth0 / enp0s3)          (eth1 / enp0s8)               │
│  10.0.2.15 (auto)         192.168.56.10                 │
│       │                         │                       │
│       │ Internet                │ Lab traffic           │
└───────┼─────────────────────────┼─────────────────────--┘
        │                         │
        ▼                         ▼
  [Host NAT Router]     [vboxnet0 Switch: 192.168.56.0/24]
        │                         │
        ▼                    ─────┼──────
  [Internet / CDN]              │
                          ┌──────┴────────────────────────┐
                          │     METASPLOITABLE 2 VM        │
                          │                                │
                          │  Adapter 1: Host-Only          │
                          │  192.168.56.20                 │
                          └────────────────────────────────┘</div>

    <h3>Configuring the Host-Only Network in VirtualBox</h3>
    <ol class="step-list">
      <li><div>In VirtualBox Manager → <strong>File → Host Network Manager</strong> (or <em>Tools → Network</em> in newer versions).</div></li>
      <li><div>Click <strong>Create</strong> — VirtualBox creates <code>vboxnet0</code> with IP <code>192.168.56.1</code> (the host's address on this network).</div></li>
      <li><div>Ensure the <strong>DHCP Server</strong> is enabled so VMs get addresses automatically, or set static IPs manually inside each VM.</div></li>
      <li><div>For <strong>Kali VM</strong>: Settings → Network → Adapter 1: NAT | Adapter 2: Host-Only Adapter → <code>vboxnet0</code>.</div></li>
      <li><div>For <strong>Target VM</strong>: Settings → Network → Adapter 1: Host-Only Adapter → <code>vboxnet0</code>. No NAT needed.</div></li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Verifying Connectivity</h3>
    <p>After starting both VMs, open a terminal in Kali and run:</p>
    <pre><code># Check Kali's IP addresses
ip addr show

# Should see:
# enp0s3: 10.0.2.15  (NAT - internet)
# enp0s8: 192.168.56.10  (Host-Only - lab)

# Ping the target to confirm lab connectivity
ping -c 4 192.168.56.20

# If you get replies, your lab network is working!</code></pre>
