  <section class="section" id="s5">
    <div class="section-tag">Attacker Machine</div>
    <h2>5. Setting Up Kali Linux — The Attacker Machine</h2>

    <h3>What Is Kali Linux?</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Kali Linux</strong> is a Debian-based Linux distribution specifically built for penetration testing and digital forensics. Maintained by Offensive Security (the organisation behind the OSCP certification), Kali comes pre-loaded with over <strong>600 security tools</strong> — everything from network scanners to password crackers to exploit frameworks. It is the de facto standard for ethical hackers and security researchers worldwide.
    </p>

    <div class="info-card accent-left">
      <h4>Brief History</h4>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>2006:</strong> BackTrack Linux released — Kali's predecessor, built on Ubuntu.</li>
        <li><strong>2013:</strong> Offensive Security rebuilt BackTrack from scratch on Debian — named it <em>Kali Linux</em>.</li>
        <li><strong>2019:</strong> Kali switches default shell from Bash to ZSH; introduces "Kali Undercover" mode (looks like Windows).</li>
        <li><strong>2023–present:</strong> Kali supports ARM, Raspberry Pi, Android (via Nethunter), and WSL2 (Windows Subsystem for Linux).</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Downloading the Kali Linux ISO</h3>
    <ol class="step-list">
      <li><div>Go to <code>kali.org/get-kali</code> — download the <strong>Installer ISO</strong> (not the Live version) for 64-bit AMD/Intel systems. Current size is approximately 4 GB.</div></li>
      <li><div>Verify the SHA256 hash of the downloaded file against the checksum published on the Kali website. This confirms the file is authentic and not tampered with.</div></li>
      <li><div>In Windows PowerShell: <code>Get-FileHash kali-linux-*.iso -Algorithm SHA256</code> — compare with the official hash.</div></li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Installing Kali in VirtualBox</h3>
    <ol class="step-list">
      <li><div>Create a new VM: Name <code>Kali-Attacker</code> · Type: Linux · Version: Debian (64-bit) · RAM: 2048–4096 MB · Disk: 20 GB VDI.</div></li>
      <li><div>Attach the Kali ISO to the optical drive. Start the VM. Choose <strong>Graphical Install</strong>.</div></li>
      <li><div>Follow prompts: language → location → keyboard → hostname (<code>kali</code>) → domain (leave blank) → create root/user password.</div></li>
      <li><div>Partition: choose <em>Guided - use entire disk</em> → all files in one partition → Finish → write changes to disk.</div></li>
      <li><div>Select a mirror for package downloads (leave default) → install GRUB bootloader → reboot.</div></li>
      <li><div>After reboot, log in with credentials you set. Install Guest Additions: Devices menu → Insert Guest Additions CD Image → follow prompts in terminal.</div></li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Essential Kali Tools — Verified &amp; Ready</h3>
    <p>After installation, verify these core tools are present (they should be by default):</p>

    <div class="compare-grid">
      <div class="info-card">
        <h5 style="color:var(--accent)">Reconnaissance</h5>
        <ul>
          <li><code>nmap</code> — network scanner &amp; port mapper</li>
          <li><code>recon-ng</code> — web reconnaissance framework</li>
          <li><code>maltego</code> — OSINT &amp; link analysis</li>
          <li><code>whois</code>, <code>dig</code>, <code>theHarvester</code></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="info-card">
        <h5 style="color:var(--accent)">Exploitation</h5>
        <ul>
          <li><code>metasploit-framework</code> — the world's most used exploit framework</li>
          <li><code>searchsploit</code> — offline exploit-db search</li>
          <li><code>sqlmap</code> — SQL injection automation</li>
          <li><code>beef-xss</code> — browser exploitation framework</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="info-card">
        <h5 style="color:var(--accent)">Password Attacks</h5>
        <ul>
          <li><code>john</code> — John the Ripper, offline cracker</li>
          <li><code>hashcat</code> — GPU-accelerated cracker</li>
          <li><code>hydra</code> — online brute-force tool</li>
          <li><code>crunch</code> — wordlist generator</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="info-card">
        <h5 style="color:var(--accent)">Network Analysis</h5>
        <ul>
          <li><code>wireshark</code> — packet capture &amp; analysis</li>
          <li><code>tcpdump</code> — CLI packet capture</li>
          <li><code>netcat</code> — raw TCP/UDP connections</li>
          <li><code>arpspoof</code> / <code>ettercap</code> — MITM attacks</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Verifying Tools After Installation</h3>
    <pre><code># Verify Metasploit is ready
msfconsole --version

# Verify nmap
nmap --version

# Verify Burp Suite (GUI tool, check it launches)
burpsuite &amp;

# Update all tools
sudo apt update &amp;&amp; sudo apt upgrade -y

# Update Metasploit specifically
sudo msfupdate</code></pre>

    <div class="info-card warning-left">
      <h4>⚠️ Important</h4>
      <p style="margin:0">Always run <code>sudo apt update</code> before starting a lab session. Tool databases (especially Metasploit's exploit modules) are updated frequently. Outdated tools may miss recent vulnerabilities or have bugs that are already fixed.</p>
    </div>
