  <section class="section" id="s6">
    <div class="section-tag">Target Machine</div>
    <h2>6. Setting Up the Target Machine</h2>
    <p>
      Your attacker needs something to attack. The golden rule: <strong>never use a real, production system as your target</strong>. Instead, use one of two purposefully vulnerable options:
    </p>

    <h3>Option A — Metasploitable 2 (Recommended for Beginners)</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Metasploitable 2</strong> is an intentionally vulnerable Linux VM created by Rapid7 (makers of Metasploit). It is riddled with security holes — outdated services, weak passwords, misconfigured applications — all by design. It is the perfect punching bag for learning exploitation.
    </p>

    <div class="info-card accent2-left">
      <h4>What Metasploitable 2 Includes</h4>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>vsftpd 2.3.4</strong> — contains a famous backdoor (CVE-2011-2523)</li>
        <li><strong>OpenSSH 4.7p1</strong> — exploitable for username enumeration</li>
        <li><strong>Apache 2.2.8 + PHP 5.2.4</strong> — multiple web vulnerabilities</li>
        <li><strong>Samba 3.0.20</strong> — CVE-2007-2447 username map script RCE</li>
        <li><strong>MySQL 5.0.51a</strong> — no root password by default</li>
        <li><strong>DVWA</strong> — Damn Vulnerable Web Application (built in)</li>
        <li><strong>Mutillidae</strong> — another vulnerable web app for web app testing</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Setting Up Metasploitable 2</h3>
    <ol class="step-list">
      <li><div>Download from <code>sourceforge.net/projects/metasploitable</code> — you get a <code>.zip</code> file containing a <code>.vmdk</code> (VMware disk image).</div></li>
      <li><div>In VirtualBox, create a new VM: Name <code>Metasploitable2-Target</code> · Type: Linux · Version: Ubuntu (64-bit) · RAM: 512–1024 MB.</div></li>
      <li><div>When prompted for a hard disk, choose <strong>Use an existing virtual hard disk file</strong> → browse to the extracted <code>.vmdk</code> file.</div></li>
      <li><div>Network: Adapter 1 → <strong>Host-Only</strong> → <code>vboxnet0</code>. <em>Do not add a NAT adapter to the target.</em></div></li>
      <li><div>Start the VM. Login: <code>msfadmin / msfadmin</code>. Run <code>ifconfig</code> to note the IP (should be in <code>192.168.56.x</code>).</div></li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Option B — Ubuntu Server (For a More Realistic Target)</h3>
    <p>
      If you want to practice hardening and attacking a more realistic machine, set up a fresh <strong>Ubuntu Server LTS</strong> VM and deliberately misconfigure it. This approach teaches you both offensive and defensive skills.
    </p>

    <ol class="step-list">
      <li><div>Download Ubuntu Server ISO from <code>ubuntu.com/download/server</code> (latest LTS, ~1.5 GB).</div></li>
      <li><div>Create VM: Name <code>Ubuntu-Target</code> · RAM: 1024 MB · Disk: 15 GB · Network: Host-Only only.</div></li>
      <li><div>Install Ubuntu Server with minimal packages. Choose your own username and password (use something weak like <code>admin/admin123</code> for practice).</div></li>
      <li><div>Intentionally install vulnerable services: <code>sudo apt install vsftpd openssh-server apache2 telnetd</code>.</div></li>
      <li><div>Misconfigure services: enable anonymous FTP, disable firewall (<code>sudo ufw disable</code>), leave default credentials.</div></li>
    </ol>

    <div class="analogy-box">
      <div class="analogy-label">🔍 Real-World Analogy</div>
      <p style="margin:0">Setting up Metasploitable 2 is like a locksmith training on a practice lock deliberately made easy. Ubuntu Server misconfigured by you is like practicing on an old real lock from a junkyard. Both are valid practice tools — neither should ever be on a real network.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Confirming the Lab is Working End-to-End</h3>
    <pre><code># From Kali, scan the target
nmap -sV -p- 192.168.56.20

# Expected output shows open ports:
# 21/tcp  open  ftp      vsftpd 2.3.4
# 22/tcp  open  ssh      OpenSSH 4.7p1
# 80/tcp  open  http     Apache httpd 2.2.8
# 139/tcp open  netbios-ssn
# 445/tcp open  microsoft-ds Samba 3.0.20
# ... and many more

# This confirms attacker and target are communicating!</code></pre>
  </section>

