  <section class="section" id="s7">
    <div class="section-tag">Safety Net</div>
    <h2>7. Snapshot Management — Your Undo Button</h2>

    <h3>What Is a Snapshot?</h3>
    <p>
      A <strong>snapshot</strong> is a complete, frozen copy of a virtual machine's state at a specific moment — including the OS state, all files on disk, and even the contents of RAM (if taken while running). Think of it as a save game in a video game. If your experiment crashes the VM, corrupts files, or installs ransomware as a demo, you simply <em>restore</em> the snapshot and everything is exactly as it was before the disaster.
    </p>

    <div class="analogy-box">
      <div class="analogy-label">🎮 Analogy</div>
      <p style="margin:0">Imagine you are exploring a dangerous dungeon in a game. Before each boss fight, you save your progress. If you die, you reload the save and try again — no permanent consequences. Snapshots work exactly the same way for your VMs. Before every major test or exploit, save a snapshot. Experiment freely. Restore if things go wrong.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Creating a Snapshot</h3>
    <ol class="step-list">
      <li><div>In VirtualBox Manager, right-click the VM → <strong>Take Snapshot</strong>. OR inside a running VM: Machine menu → Take Snapshot.</div></li>
      <li><div>Give it a descriptive name: <code>Kali-Fresh-Install-2025</code> or <code>Metasploitable-Clean-Baseline</code>.</div></li>
      <li><div>Add a description: "Before Metasploit practice session 1 — all services running."</div></li>
      <li><div>Click OK. VirtualBox saves the state in seconds (or minutes for large VMs).</div></li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Restoring a Snapshot</h3>
    <ol class="step-list">
      <li><div>In VirtualBox Manager, click the VM → click the <strong>Snapshots</strong> tab (hamburger menu on newer versions).</div></li>
      <li><div>Right-click the snapshot you want → <strong>Restore Snapshot</strong>.</div></li>
      <li><div>VirtualBox asks if you want to save the current state first — usually choose <em>No</em> (discard current changes).</div></li>
      <li><div>The VM reverts in seconds. Start it normally — it is exactly as it was when you took the snapshot.</div></li>
    </ol>

