    <h3>When to Choose VMware Over VirtualBox</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>You need to run resource-intensive VMs and performance is critical.</li>
      <li>You are preparing for VMware-based certifications or work in an enterprise that uses vSphere/ESXi.</li>
      <li>You need to import <code>.ova</code> or <code>.ovf</code> templates directly (common in enterprise lab distributions).</li>
      <li>You are on macOS and need Apple Silicon (M1/M2/M3) support — VMware Fusion has better ARM support than VirtualBox historically.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Setting Up Kali on VMware Player</h3>
    <p>Offensive Security provides a pre-built Kali Linux VMware image at <code>kali.org/get-kali/#kali-virtual-machines</code>. This is a <code>.7z</code> archive containing a <code>.vmx</code> configuration file and a <code>.vmdk</code> virtual disk. Simply extract and open in VMware — no installation wizard required. Network configuration concepts are identical to VirtualBox: use VMnet1 (Host-Only equivalent) for your lab network.</p>
  </section>

