    <h2>10. Advantages, Disadvantages &amp; Putting It All Together</h2>

    <h3>Advantages of a Virtual Home Lab</h3>
    <div class="compare-grid">
      <div class="compare-card pro info-card">
        <h5>Advantages</h5>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Zero cost:</strong> VirtualBox, Kali, Metasploitable 2, and Ubuntu Server are all free.</li>
          <li><strong>Safe and legal:</strong> You own all machines in your lab — attack away.</li>
          <li><strong>Infinitely repeatable:</strong> Snapshots mean you can redo any exercise.</li>
          <li><strong>Portable:</strong> Copy VM files to an external drive and run your lab anywhere.</li>
          <li><strong>Multiple simultaneous environments:</strong> Run 3–4 VMs if your hardware allows.</li>
          <li><strong>Mirrors real-world setups:</strong> Enterprise networks are virtualised — your lab mirrors real infrastructure.</li>
          <li><strong>Skill development:</strong> Direct hands-on experience beats watching videos.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-card con info-card">
        <h5>Disadvantages</h5>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Hardware limitations:</strong> Running multiple VMs needs significant RAM and CPU.</li>
          <li><strong>Performance:</strong> Type 2 hypervisors have overhead — VMs are slower than bare metal.</li>
          <li><strong>Storage hungry:</strong> VM disks and snapshots consume tens of GBs quickly.</li>
          <li><strong>Setup complexity:</strong> Network misconfiguration can be confusing for beginners.</li>
          <li><strong>Not identical to real environments:</strong> Some hardware-level attacks can't be replicated in VMs.</li>
          <li><strong>Learning curve:</strong> Linux command-line knowledge required for most security work.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Complete Home Lab Setup Checklist</h3>
    <div class="info-card accent2-left">
      <ol style="color:var(--text-secondary); font-size:0.95rem; padding-left:18px;">
