    <h3>What to Practice First</h3>
    <ol class="step-list">
      <li><div><strong>Reconnaissance:</strong> Run <code>nmap -sV -O 192.168.56.20</code> to enumerate all services and OS details on Metasploitable.</div></li>
      <li><div><strong>Vulnerability research:</strong> Take the service versions from nmap, search <code>searchsploit vsftpd 2.3.4</code> — learn what CVEs exist.</div></li>
      <li><div><strong>First exploit:</strong> Use Metasploit to exploit the vsftpd backdoor: <code>use exploit/unix/ftp/vsftpd_234_backdoor</code>. Get a root shell.</div></li>
      <li><div><strong>Web app testing:</strong> Open Kali's browser, navigate to <code>http://192.168.56.20/dvwa</code> — practice SQL injection and XSS in DVWA.</div></li>
      <li><div><strong>Password attacks:</strong> Use Hydra to brute-force SSH: <code>hydra -l msfadmin -P /usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt ssh://192.168.56.20</code>.</div></li>
      <li><div><strong>Capture the flag:</strong> Sign up for HackTheBox or TryHackMe — they provide additional VMs (via VPN) to practice on legally.</div></li>
    </ol>

    <div class="highlight-box">
      <strong>💡 Pro Tip:</strong> Keep a lab journal — a text file or Obsidian vault where you document every step, every command, and every output. Reviewing your journal after each session accelerates learning 3× faster than practice alone. Many security professionals build their entire portfolio from lab journals.
